n. 1. a metric unit of length equal to one ten billionth of a meter (or 0.0001 micron); used to specify wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation
n. 2. any of several fat-soluble vitamins essential for normal vision; prevents night blindness or inflammation or dryness of the eyes
n. 3. one of the four nucleotides used in building DNA; all four nucleotides have a common phosphate group and a sugar (ribose)
n. 4. (biochemistry) purine base found in DNA and RNA; pairs with thymine in DNA and with uracil in RNA
n. 5. the basic unit of electric current adopted under the Systeme International d'Unites; "a typical household circuit carries 15 to 50 amps"
n. 6. the 1st letter of the Roman alphabet
n. 7. the blood group whose red cells carry the A antigenn. 1. the trait of lacking restraint or control; reckless freedom from inhibition or worry; "she danced with abandon"
n. 2. a feeling of extreme emotional intensity; "the wildness of his anger"
v. 1. forsake, leave behind; "We abandoned the old car in the empty parking lot"
v. 2. give up with the intent of never claiming again; "Abandon your life to God"; "She gave up her children to her ex-husband when she moved to Tahiti"; "We gave the drowning victim up for dead"
v. 3. leave behind empty; move out of; "You must vacate your office by tonight"
v. 4. stop maintaining or insisting on; of ideas or claims; "He abandoned the thought of asking for her hand in marriage"; "Both sides have to give up some claims in these negotiations"
v. 5. leave someone who needs or counts on you; leave in the lurch; "The mother deserted her children"adj. 1. forsaken by owner or inhabitants ; "weed-grown yard of an abandoned farmhouse"
adj. 2. free from constraint; "an abandoned sadness born of grief"- Liam O'Flahertyn. 1. an interruption in the intensity or amount of something
n. 2. the act of abating; "laws enforcing noise abatement"n. 1. the superior of an abbey of monksn. 1. a town in western Washington
n. 2. a town in northeastern South Dakota
