adv. 3. in a place across an oceanadj. 1. marked by sudden changes in subject and sharp transitions; "abrupt prose"
adj. 2. exceedingly sudden and unexpected; "came to an abrupt stop"; "an abrupt change in the weather"
adj. 3. extremely steep; "an abrupt canyon"; "the precipitous rapids of the upper river"; "the precipitous hills of Chinese paintings"; "a sharp drop"
adj. 4. surprisingly and unceremoniously brusque in manner; "an abrupt reply"adv. 1. quickly and without warning; "he stopped suddenly"n. 1. an abrupt discourteous manner
n. 2. the property possessed by a slope that is very steep
n. 3. the quality of happening with headlong haste or without warningn. 1. the state of being absent; "he was surprised by the absence of any explanation"
n. 2. failure to be present
n. 3. the time interval during which something or somebody is away; "he visited during my absence"
n. 4. the occurrence of an abrupt, transient loss or impairment of consciousness (which is not subsequently remembered), sometimes with light twitching, fluttering eyelids, etc.; common in petit mal epilepsyv. 1. go away or leave; "He absented himself"
adj. 1. not being in a specified place
adj. 2. nonexistent; "the thumb is absent"; "her appetite was lacking"
adj. 3. lost in thought; showing preoccupation; "an absent stare"; "an absentminded professor"; "the scatty glancing quality of a hyperactive but unfocused intelligence"n. 1. something that is conceived or that exists independently and not in relation to other things; something that does not depend on anything else and is beyond human control; something that is not relative; "no mortal being can influence the absolute"
adj. 1. perfect or complete or pure; "absolute loyalty"; "absolute silence"; "absolute truth"; "absolute alcohol"
adj. 2. complete and without restriction or qualification; sometimes used informally as intensifiers; "absolute freedom"; "an absolute dimwit"; "a downright lie"; "out-and-out mayhem"; "an out-and-out lie"; "a rank outsider"; "many right-down vices"; "got the job through sheer persistence"; "sheer stupidity"
adj. 3. not limited by law; "an absolute monarch"
adj. 4. expressing finality with no implication of possible change; "an absolute guarantee to respect the nation's authority"
adj. 5. not capable of being violated or infringed; "infrangible human rights"adv. 1. completely and without qualification; used informally as intensifiers; "an absolutely magnificent painting"; "a perfectly idiotic idea"; "you're perfectly right"; "utterly miserable"; "you can be dead sure of my innocence"; "was dead tired"; "dead right"
adv. 2. totally and definitely; without question; "we are absolutely opposed to the idea"; "he forced himself to lie absolutely still"; "iron is absolutely necessary"v. 1. grant remission of a sin to; "The priest absolved him and told him to say ten Hail Mary's"
v. 2. let off the hook; "I absolve you from this responsibility"adj. 1. freed from any question of guilt; "is absolved from all blame"; "was now clear of the charge of cowardice"; "his official honor is vindicated"v. 1. become imbued; "The liquids, light, and gases absorb"
v. 2. take up mentally; "he absorbed the knowledge or beliefs of his tribe"
