v. 1. rid of obstructions; "Clear your desk"
v. 2. make a way or path by removing objects; "Clear a path through the dense forest"
v. 3. become clear; "The sky cleared after the storm"
v. 4. grant authorization or clearance for; "Clear the manuscript for publication"; "The rock star never authorized this slanderous biography"
v. 5. remove; "clear the leaves from the lawn"; "Clear snow from the road"
v. 6. go unchallenged; be approved; "The bill cleared the House"
v. 7. be debited and credited to the proper bank accounts; "The check will clear within 2 business days"
v. 8. go away or disappear; "The fog cleared in the afternoon"
v. 9. pass by, over, or under without making contact; "the balloon cleared the tree tops"
v. 10. make free from confusion or ambiguity; make clear; "Could you clarify these remarks?"; "Clear up the question of who is at fault"
v. 11. free from payment of customs duties, as of a shipment; "Clear the ship and let it dock"
v. 12. clear from impurities, blemishes, pollution, etc.; "clear the water before it can be drunk"
v. 13. yield as a net profit; "This sale netted me $1 million"
v. 14. make as a net profit; "The company cleared $1 million"
v. 15. earn on some commercial or business transaction; earn as salary or wages; "How much do you make a month in your new job?"; "She earns a lot in her new job"; "this merger brought in lots of money"; "He clears $5,000 each month"
v. 16. sell; "We cleared a lot of the old model cars"
v. 17. pass an inspection or receive authorization; "clear customs"
v. 18. pronounce not guilty of criminal charges; "The suspect was cleared of the murder charges"
v. 19. settle, as of a debt; "clear a debt"; "solve an old debt"
v. 20. make clear, bright, light, or translucent; "The water had to be cleared through filtering"
v. 21. rid of instructions or data; "clear a memory buffer"
v. 22. remove (people) from a building; "clear the patrons from the theater after the bomb threat"
v. 23. remove the occupants of; "Clear the building"
v. 24. free (the throat) by making a rasping sound; "Clear the throat"
adj. 1. readily apparent to the mind; "a clear and present danger"; "a clear explanation"; "a clear case of murder"; "a clear indication that she was angry"; "gave us a clear idea of human nature"
adj. 2. free from confusion or doubt; "a complex problem requiring a clear head"; "not clear about what is expected of us"
adj. 3. affording free passage or view; "a clear view"; "a clear path to victory"; "open waters"; "the open countryside"
adj. 4. allowing light to pass through; "clear water"; "clear plastic bags"; "clear glass"; "the air is clear and clean"
adj. 5. free from contact or proximity or connection; "we were clear of the danger"; "the ship was clear of the reef"
adj. 6. characterized by freedom from troubling thoughts (especially guilt); "a clear conscience"; "regarded her questioner with clear untroubled eyes"
adj. 7. (of sound or color) free from anything that dulls or dims; "efforts to obtain a clean bass in orchestral recordings"; "clear laughter like a waterfall"; "clear reds and blues"; "a light lilting voice like a silver bell"
adj. 8. (especially of a title) free from any encumbrance or limitation that presents a question of fact or law; "I have clear title to this property"
adj. 9. clear and distinct to the senses; easily perceptible; "as clear as a whistle"; "clear footprints in the snow"; "the letter brought back a clear image of his grandfather"; "a spire clean-cut against the sky"; "a clear-cut pattern"
adj. 10. accurately stated or described; "a set of well-defined values"
adj. 11. free from clouds or mist or haze; "on a clear day"
adj. 12. free of restrictions or qualifications; "a clean bill of health"; "a clear winner"
adj. 13. free from flaw or blemish or impurity; "a clear perfect diamond"; "the clear complexion of a healthy young woman"
adj. 14. clear of charges or deductions; "a clear profit"
adj. 15. easily deciphered
adj. 16. freed from any question of guilt; "is absolved from all blame"; "was now clear of the charge of cowardice"; "his official honor is vindicated"
adj. 17. characterized by ease and quickness in perceiving; "clear mind"; "a percipient author"
