v. 1. go across or through; "We passed the point where the police car had parked"; "A terrible thought went through his mind"
v. 2. move past; "A black limousine passed by when she looked out the window"; "He passed his professor in the hall"; "One line of soldiers surpassed the other"
v. 3. make laws, bills, etc. or bring into effect by legislation; "They passed the amendment"; "We cannot legislate how people spend their free time"
v. 4. pass by; "three years elapsed"
v. 5. place into the hands or custody of; "hand me the spoon, please"; "Turn the files over to me, please"; "He turned over the prisoner to his lawyers"
v. 6. stretch out over a distance, space, time, or scope; run or extend between two points or beyond a certain point; "Service runs all the way to Cranbury"; "His knowledge doesn't go very far"; "My memory extends back to my fourth year of life"; "The facts extend beyond a consideration of her personal assets"
v. 7. travel past; "The sports car passed all the trucks"
v. 8. come to pass; "What is happening?"; "The meeting took place off without an incidence"; "Nothing occurred that seemed important"
v. 9. go unchallenged; be approved; "The bill cleared the House"
v. 10. pass time in a specific way; "how are you spending your summer vacation?"
v. 11. pass over, across, or through; "He ran his eyes over her body"; "She ran her fingers along the carved figurine"; "He drew her hair through his fingers"
v. 12. transmit information ; "Please communicate this message to all employees"; "pass along the good news"
v. 13. disappear gradually; "The pain eventually passed off"
v. 14. go successfully through a test or a selection process; "She passed the new Jersey Bar Exam and can practice law now"
v. 15. be superior or better than some standard; "She exceeded our expectations"; "She topped her performance of last year"
v. 16. accept or judge as acceptable; "The teacher passed the student although he was weak"
v. 17. allow to go without comment or censure; "the insult passed as if unnoticed"
v. 18. transfer to another; of rights or property; "Our house passed under his official control"
v. 19. pass into a specified state or condition; "He sank into nirvana"
v. 20. throw (a ball) to another player; "Smith passed"
v. 21. be inherited by; "The estate fell to my sister"; "The land returned to the family"; "The estate devolved to an heir that everybody had assumed to be dead"
v. 22. cause to pass; "She passed around the plates"
v. 23. grant authorization or clearance for; "Clear the manuscript for publication"; "The rock star never authorized this slanderous biography"
v. 24. pass from physical life and lose all bodily attributes and functions necessary to sustain life; "She died from cancer"; "The children perished in the fire"; "The patient went peacefully"; "The old guy kicked the bucket at the age of 102"
v. 25. eliminate from the body; "Pass a kidney stone"
