n. 8. activity by children that is guided more by imagination than by fixed rules; "Freud believed in the utility of play to a small child"
n. 9. (in games or plays or other performances) the time during which play proceeds; "rain stopped play in the 4th inning"
n. 10. the removal of constraints; "he gave free rein to his impulses"; "they gave full play to the artist's talent"
n. 11. a weak and tremulous light; "the shimmer of colors on iridescent feathers"; "the play of light on the water"
n. 12. verbal wit or mockery (often at another's expense but not to be taken seriously); "he became a figure of fun"; "he said it in sport"
n. 13. movement or space for movement; "there was too much play in the steering wheel"
n. 14. gay or light-hearted recreational activity for diversion or amusement; "it was all done in play"; "their frolic in the surf threatened to become ugly"
n. 15. (game) the activity of doing something in an agreed succession; "it is my turn"; "it is still my play"
n. 16. the act of playing for stakes in the hope of winning (including the payment of a price for a chance to win a prize); "his gambling cost him a fortune"; "there was heavy play at the blackjack table"
n. 17. the act using a sword (or other weapon) vigorously and skillfully
v. 1. participate in games or sport; "We played hockey all afternoon"; "play cards"; "Pele played for the Brazilian teams in many important matches"
v. 2. act or have an effect in a specified way or with a specific effect or outcome; "This factor played only a minor part in his decision"; "This development played into her hands"; "I played no role in your dismissal"
v. 3. play on an instrument; "The band played all night long"
v. 4. play a role or part; "Gielgud played Hamlet"; "She wants to act Lady Macbeth, but she is too young for the role"; "She played the servant to her husband's master"
v. 5. be at play; be engaged in playful activity; amuse oneself in a way characteristic of children; "The kids were playing outside all day"; "I used to play with trucks as a little girl"
v. 6. replay (as a melody); "Play it again, Sam"; "She played the third movement very beautifully"
v. 7. perform music on (a musical instrument); "He plays the flute"; "Can you play on this old recorder?"
v. 8. pretend to have certain qualities or state of mind; "He acted the idiot"; "She plays deaf when the news are bad"
v. 9. move or seem to move quickly, lightly, or irregularly; "The spotlights played on the politicians"
v. 10. bet or wager (money); "He played $20 on the new horse"; "She plays the races"
v. 11. engage in recreational activities rather than work; occupy oneself in a diversion; "On weekends I play"; "The students all recreate alike"
v. 12. pretend to be somebody in the framework of a game or playful activity; "Let's play like I am mommy"; "Play cowboy and Indians"
v. 13. emit recorded sound; "The tape was playing for hours"; "the stereo was playing Beethoven when I entered"
v. 14. perform on a certain location; "The prodigy played Carnegie Hall at the age of 16"; "She has been playing on Broadway for years"
v. 15. put (a card or piece) into play during a game, or act strategically as if in a card game; "He is playing his cards close to his chest"; "The Democrats still have some cards to play before they will concede the electoral victory"
v. 16. engage in an activity as if it were a game rather than take it seriously; "They played games on their opponents"; "play the stock market"; "play with her feelings"; "toy with an idea"
v. 17. behave in a certain way; "play safe"; "play it safe"; "play fair"
v. 18. cause to emit recorded audio or video; "They ran the tapes over and over again"; "I'll play you my favorite record"; "He never tires of playing that video"
v. 19. manipulate manually or in one's mind or imagination; "She played nervously with her wedding ring"; "Don't fiddle with the screws"; "He played with the idea of running for the Senate"
v. 20. use to one's advantage; "She plays on her clients' emotions"
v. 21. consider not very seriously; "He is trifling with her"; "She plays with the thought of moving to Tasmania"
v. 22. be received or accepted or interpreted in a specific way; "This speech didn't play well with the American public"; "His remarks played to the suspicions of the committee"
v. 23. behave carelessly or indifferently; "Play about with a young girl's affection"
v. 24. cause to move or operate freely within a bounded space; "The engine has a wheel that is playing in a rack"
v. 25. perform on a stage or theater; "She acts in this play"; "He acted in `Julius Caesar'"; "I played in `A Christmas Carol'"
v. 26. be performed or presented for public viewing; "What's playing in the local movie theater?"; "`Cats' has been playing on Broadway for many years"
